[SNIP]
>
> Now here's the fun part - If the item about to be fooproofed
> is already more than 1 point damaged

There's no such thing as "more than 1 point damaged" for base ac:1
armor.  Even if it's thoroughly rusty and thoroughly corroded (6 total
"steps" of damage), it's only 1 point of AC that you lose.

> > Now with base ac2 items, the situation is slightly different.  But
> > there's only 1 of those (a robe) that isn't very easy to replace.  The
> > robe is sometimes worth fooproofing (esp. if all your stuff is better
> > than +1 already)  Dwarvish helms, iron shoes, and large shields are
> > abundant.
>
> So ?oEA are wasted on those items IF you have a good chance
> of getting better items soon.

Actually, they're wasted if you have a good chance of getting items
that are no more than 1 point worse soon (by reasoning similar to that
for base ac:1 armor).  So if you could soon get, say, an orcish helm or
an elvish leather helm (let alone another dwarven helm) then
fooproofing your dwarven helm is wasted.  Enchant your gloves or robe
or whatever instead.

For a robe, which is not easily replaced, fooproofing is not a waste if
your other armor is all enchanted to a point where the average
enchantment is less than +2, but in reality it's got to be somewhere
south of that since "robe getting damaged twice" is presumably going to
happen some number of turns later than now.

> > Except for robes (where grabbing a replacement isn't trivially easy), I
> > can't imagine that giving up at least one point of AC throughout the
> > game is worth that minimal tradeoff, especially since if you get 2-3
> > points of AC then you're still at least as well off even in that window
> > of time.
> >
> > Now using c?destroy armor to fooproof is worthwile.
>
> Unless the item is already damaged, that is.  If it already has 2-3
> points of damage then the bonus is as good as a blessed ?oEA.

Nothing useful gets 3 points of damage (we're talking about a monk
here, but even for other classes the only body armor worth spending any
time improving is DSM or mithril, neither of which rust/corrode/burn at
all).

But yeah, using c?destroy armor to fooproof is reserve for items that
haven't been damaged (or at least not damaged fully).  Those that had
been damaged should just be left damaged (replaced if they're easily
replaced, otherwise left as is) or if you ever get to the point of
having surplus ?ea you could use those to fix things up, or if it's a
robe that has 2 damage you might consider it when all your armor's at
+3 already (and so the average value of enchanting is less than +2).

You could also #dip rusted items in oil and fooproof them with
c?destroy armor.



